at is covered. The brief pointed the red team at the rescan question gaze had already reproduced, so its finding 1 confirms gaze's finding rather than discovering it independently.\n\nFindings:\n1. blocking (regression check and redteam, both reproduced): scripts/gaze_seat.py:518 and :533. A secret that comes after a quoted secret on the same line is no longer redacted. The `rest` group `[^\\n]*` consumes the whole remaining line. `_redact` hands the text after the closing quote back unchanged, and `re.sub` resumes after the consumed match, so that text is never scanned again.\n   - `token=\"FAKEa1\" password=\"FAKEb2\"`: head gives `<redacted> password=\"FAKEb2\"`, main gives `<redacted> <redacted>`.\n   - `{\"token\":\"FAKEa1\",\"password\":\"FAKEb2\"}`: head gives `{\"<redacted>,\"password\":\"FAKEb2\"}`, main gives `{\"<redacted>`.\n   - `api_key: \"FAKEa1\" then secret=FAKEb2` and `password='FAKEa1' token=FAKEb2`: head leaks `FAKEb2` in both.\n   - In all, 6 of the 14 check shapes leak on head and not on main.\n   - scenario: a seat's stderr or a quoted seat body logs a JSON object or a header line with two credentials. The second one lands in refs/notes/review, which is pushed.\n   - on main: no. origin/main redacts every one of these inputs.\n   - Suggested fix: keep the design and resume the scan where the closed value ends. One way is a `finditer`/`search(text, pos)` loop in scrub() that continues at the position after the closing quote, instead of returning the tail from the replacement. Another is to scrub `rest[i+1:]` with the same pattern, which is also linear. Add a test for two quoted secrets on one line and one for compact JSON with two secret keys.\n\nThe intent is met otherwise:\n- All the earlier quote shapes are fully redacted: an escaped quote inside an escaped value, an apostrophe or `\\\"` inside a value, single quotes around `\"`, and an unterminated quote. On the unterminated shape main leaked `FAKEsecret123`.\n- Compact-JSON later fields survive when they are not secrets: `{\"token\":\"FAKEa1\",\"model\":\"x\"}` gives `{\"<redacted>,\"model\":\"x\"}`.\n- The scrub no longer crosses a line: `access_token:\\nreview plan:` comes out intact.\n- The scanner is linear. A run of 16 000, 160 000 and 1 600 000 backslashes took 0.0024 s, 0.024 s and 0.23 s (redteam), and 20 000 took 0.003 s (check). These timings were taken on padme, which shares its cores with other jobs.\n\nconsider (redteam, not adopted): scripts/gaze_seat.py:513. A bare `Basic YTpiYw==` (6 characters before the padding) is no longer redacted, while main redacted it. This comes from round 2's deliberate floor of 8 characters for a bare Basic, which spares `basic usage: run`. A Basic value right after an Authorization label is still redacted at any length. It is outside this round's what.\n\nnofollow (the same on main, so pre-existing): scrub() as a whole is still quadratic on a long unbroken word run. At n=20 000 it takes about 1.1 s on both heads, almost all of it in the email pattern (SCRUB_PATTERNS[0]). The new scanner adds nothing measurable.\n\nTargeted tests: selected: tests/test_forge_offline.py tests/test_gaze_close.py tests/test_gaze_read_only.py tests/test_gaze_seat.py tests/test_gaze_wall_endpoints.py tests/test_review_panel_cleanup_contract.py; fallback: none; result: pass (6 selected file(s)), 197 passed. None of the tests covers a line with two secrets.\n\nRounds: this is round 4, and the first round on the option-C design. The quote handling holds against every earlier shape. The new blocker is in how the scanner hands back to `re.sub` (where the scan resumes), not in the quote logic, so it does not call for another redesign. A one-line resume fix plus two tests should close it.\n\nUnresolved simplify: not planned\n\nduration: 4m27s\ntelemetry: wall=na agents=1 tokens=na cost~=$na\nseat: redteam \u00b7 runtime=codex \u00b7 family=openai \u00b7 route=gpt-6.1-sol \u00b7 model=openai/gpt-6.1-sol \u00b7 status: ran\nunresolved_rev